WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.710
This is the narrated briefing for a
companion guide in the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.784
Workbook: Global Secure Access and
Zero Trust Network Engineering,

4
00:00:09.784 --> 00:00:11.312
an implementation guide.

5
00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.603
One thing first: the guide is a draft,

6
00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:17.474
awaiting the editor's review,
so recheck it after the review.

7
00:00:17.474 --> 00:00:19.625
Its idea fits in a sentence.

8
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:22.444
A V P N proves that a
device reached the network;

9
00:00:22.444 --> 00:00:26.449
the work in this guide makes every
connection prove who is asking,

10
00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:30.825
from which device, for which application,
and leaves a record that it did.

11
00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:34.212
The guide assumes a
civilian agency at fed-ramp

12
00:00:34.212 --> 00:00:37.338
Moderate on Microsoft
three sixty-five G C C.

13
00:00:37.338 --> 00:00:41.968
Its product facts come from Microsoft
Learn, its tick three point oh

14
00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:47.012
statements from CISA's own documents,
all read on November eleventh, 2026.

15
00:00:47.012 --> 00:00:49.151
Nothing in it was run against a tenant.

16
00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:53.000
We'll go in four parts:
why, what, who, and how.

17
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.900
Why does this matter?

18
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.477
Global Secure Access is Microsoft's security
service edge: Entra Internet Access

19
00:01:03.477 --> 00:01:08.562
and Entra Private Access, used as the
agency's identity-aware network layer.

20
00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:12.377
It carries three kinds of traffic,
each with its own profile:

21
00:01:12.377 --> 00:01:16.113
Microsoft traffic, internet
traffic, and private traffic.

22
00:01:16.113 --> 00:01:21.188
Conditional Access decides, the service
edge enforces, and the logs prove it.

23
00:01:21.188 --> 00:01:23.438
Two facts shape everything after.

24
00:01:23.438 --> 00:01:28.717
Learn's known limitations page says
the service is available in G C C,

25
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:33.713
but isn't yet supported in G C C High,
D O D, or other sovereign clouds.

26
00:01:33.713 --> 00:01:37.202
And Microsoft's Graph network
access A P I's are in beta,

27
00:01:37.202 --> 00:01:41.802
which Learn says is subject to change
and not supported in production,

28
00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:45.926
so the guide runs them by hand
and keeps each result as evidence.

29
00:01:45.926 --> 00:01:51.136
The twelve steps run in a set order, so
the logs exist before anything is enforced,

30
00:01:51.136 --> 00:01:54.260
and nothing blocks before its path exists.

31
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.110
Where does this sit in federal terms?

32
00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.097
Tick three point oh is technology-neutral:
it sets goals, not products.

33
00:02:05.097 --> 00:02:08.414
CISA's Remote User Use Case
has three security patterns,

34
00:02:08.414 --> 00:02:12.648
and each has an option that runs through
a security-as-a-service provider.

35
00:02:12.648 --> 00:02:16.568
In the program's reading, Private
Access is that option for the campus,

36
00:02:16.568 --> 00:02:21.314
the Microsoft traffic profile with the
compliant network check is the option for a

37
00:02:21.314 --> 00:02:25.440
sanctioned cloud provider, and Internet
Access is the option for the web,

38
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.710
which CISA calls the riskiest pattern.

39
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:32.820
For branches, remote networks get only
the baseline profile and no Conditional

40
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.698
Access, so parity between a branch user
and a client user is a design question.

41
00:02:37.698 --> 00:02:41.097
The part a design most
often loses is telemetry.

42
00:02:41.097 --> 00:02:45.953
The use case says CISA's visibility
must be preserved, so agree the path

43
00:02:45.953 --> 00:02:50.561
with the agency's tick delegates before
the V P N's web path is retired.

44
00:02:50.561 --> 00:02:54.443
And none of this makes Global
Secure Access a tick product:

45
00:02:54.443 --> 00:02:56.750
CISA certifies no products.

46
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.737
Most agencies already hold a
fed-ramp authorized S S E or V P N,

47
00:03:04.737 --> 00:03:10.012
so the question is usually which functions
to move, not which vendor to buy.

48
00:03:10.012 --> 00:03:15.385
Global Secure Access decides policy
in Entra Conditional Access, the same

49
00:03:15.385 --> 00:03:20.584
engine as sign-in; a third party uses
its own engine, usually fed by Entra I D.

50
00:03:20.584 --> 00:03:23.791
Global Secure Access
reaches G C C only today;

51
00:03:23.791 --> 00:03:28.037
several third-party offerings
hold fed-ramp High listings.

52
00:03:28.037 --> 00:03:32.334
Three signals only Entra can produce:
the compliant network check,

53
00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:36.162
universal tenant restrictions,
and source I P restoration.

54
00:03:36.162 --> 00:03:37.775
Running both is supported.

55
00:03:37.775 --> 00:03:42.912
Private apps in A W S, Google Cloud or
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure are reached like

56
00:03:42.912 --> 00:03:47.596
on-premises ones, through connectors
with line of sight to the application,

57
00:03:47.596 --> 00:03:50.013
one connector group per cloud network.

58
00:03:50.013 --> 00:03:53.810
Learn has no procedure specific
to any of those clouds.

59
00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.048
Here are the twelve steps.

60
00:03:59.048 --> 00:04:02.603
One: a decision record per
function, with licenses,

61
00:04:02.603 --> 00:04:05.898
a dated availability
quote, and exit criteria.

62
00:04:05.898 --> 00:04:09.628
Two: the reference architecture
written down for this tenant,

63
00:04:09.628 --> 00:04:11.384
with a configuration export.

64
00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.435
Three: the client on every managed device.

65
00:04:14.435 --> 00:04:19.185
Four: the Microsoft traffic profile,
with source I P restoration.

66
00:04:19.185 --> 00:04:22.797
Five: internet traffic,
through security profiles.

67
00:04:22.797 --> 00:04:28.273
Six: private applications, each with
its own segments, group and policy.

68
00:04:28.273 --> 00:04:30.685
Seven: the compliant network check.

69
00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:32.598
Eight: tenant restrictions.

70
00:04:32.598 --> 00:04:35.110
Nine: T L S inspection.

71
00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.185
Ten: privileged protocols.

72
00:04:37.185 --> 00:04:41.123
Eleven: coexistence and
migration, ring by ring.

73
00:04:41.123 --> 00:04:43.684
Twelve: telemetry and evidence.

74
00:04:43.684 --> 00:04:46.717
They run in a different
order: logging first,

75
00:04:46.717 --> 00:04:50.440
and internet filtering
and T L S inspection last.

76
00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.465
What's available where?

77
00:04:55.465 --> 00:05:00.250
Learn's statement covers the
whole product: available in G C C,

78
00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.940
not yet supported in G C C High,
D O D, or other sovereign clouds.

79
00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:10.062
No feature page narrows that, so the G
C C column rests on that one sentence,

80
00:05:10.062 --> 00:05:14.415
and a preview feature's G C C
availability is not yet specified.

81
00:05:14.415 --> 00:05:16.527
Licensing follows the profiles.

82
00:05:16.527 --> 00:05:21.852
The Microsoft traffic profile, and with
it source I P restoration, the compliant

83
00:05:21.852 --> 00:05:25.241
network check and universal
tenant restrictions,

84
00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:27.339
comes with Entra I D P one or P two.

85
00:05:27.339 --> 00:05:31.053
Internet Access and Private
Access are separately licensed,

86
00:05:31.053 --> 00:05:32.728
or come with the Entra Suite.

87
00:05:32.728 --> 00:05:37.902
Remote networks need at least fifty P one
and Internet Access licenses combined.

88
00:05:37.902 --> 00:05:42.693
One conflict: Graph's settings pages
list U S Government L four and L five,

89
00:05:42.693 --> 00:05:45.616
but the product page
says not yet supported.

90
00:05:45.616 --> 00:05:47.634
The guide follows the product page,

91
00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:51.115
and the question goes to the
agency's Microsoft account team.

92
00:05:51.115 --> 00:05:54.340
Re-read the limitations
page before each ring.

93
00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.140
The reference architecture
has seven zones and planes.

94
00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.165
Managed Windows endpoints open a tunnel
to the service edge after Entra sign-in.

95
00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:11.777
Branches send traffic over an I P sec
tunnel, with no user identity on it.

96
00:06:11.777 --> 00:06:16.565
The control plane, Conditional Access,
decides and never carries traffic.

97
00:06:16.565 --> 00:06:21.510
The policy plane holds the traffic
forwarding profiles, security profiles,

98
00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:25.278
T L S policies and signaling,
and configures the data plane.

99
00:06:25.278 --> 00:06:29.866
The data plane is Microsoft's service
edge in commercial Azure regions,

100
00:06:29.866 --> 00:06:32.666
and it holds the agency's
traffic in transit.

101
00:06:32.666 --> 00:06:37.263
The agency's private network holds
connector groups, private D N S,

102
00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:42.202
applications and domain controllers
with sensors, all outbound-only.

103
00:06:42.202 --> 00:06:46.303
And the evidence zone is the Log
Analytics workspace and Sentinel.

104
00:06:46.303 --> 00:06:47.940
D N S stays split.

105
00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.495
The client doesn't support
D N S over H T T P S,

106
00:06:51.495 --> 00:06:56.880
so secure D N S in browsers is
turned off on managed devices.

107
00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:01.468
Now, who.

108
00:07:01.468 --> 00:07:06.962
Microsoft's operations guide gives a RACI
with a service owner, network security

109
00:07:06.962 --> 00:07:12.129
engineer, identity engineer, S O C analyst,
help desk and platform operations.

110
00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:15.666
The guide maps those to six
roles and adds the assessor,

111
00:07:15.666 --> 00:07:17.630
who is independent of the work.

112
00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.398
The policy owner, the service
owner with the I S S O consulted,

113
00:07:21.398 --> 00:07:26.703
is accountable for the decision record,
the architecture, the traffic profiles, the

114
00:07:26.703 --> 00:07:31.855
Conditional Access policies and their
exclusions, and the T L S inspection scope.

115
00:07:31.855 --> 00:07:35.486
The platform owner, the network
security and identity engineers,

116
00:07:35.486 --> 00:07:37.368
is responsible for building them.

117
00:07:37.368 --> 00:07:42.556
Operators run the client rings, the
connectors and the domain controller sensors.

118
00:07:42.556 --> 00:07:46.893
The security monitor owns alerts,
log freshness and the metrics.

119
00:07:46.893 --> 00:07:49.642
The help desk takes tier-one user issues.

120
00:07:49.642 --> 00:07:54.297
The assessor assesses the evidence and
recommends a finding ready for closure,

121
00:07:54.297 --> 00:07:56.554
and the authorizing official decides.

122
00:07:56.554 --> 00:08:00.140
Learn's advice is at least
two people for every role.

123
00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:04.715
Now how.

124
00:08:04.715 --> 00:08:10.603
Step one decides, per function, because
the gates differ: Microsoft traffic,

125
00:08:10.603 --> 00:08:14.402
internet, private and
branch can each be adopted,

126
00:08:14.402 --> 00:08:18.865
kept on the current V P N or S S
E, run side by side, or deferred.

127
00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:22.327
Read the tenant's licenses
and onboarding state first.

128
00:08:22.327 --> 00:08:27.787
The decision record, the guide's second
artifact, holds a dated availability quote,

129
00:08:27.787 --> 00:08:32.603
the license count, compensating
controls, exit criteria and an approver.

130
00:08:32.603 --> 00:08:38.432
In G C C High or D O D, don't build: record
defer, and keep a compensating path,

131
00:08:38.432 --> 00:08:43.960
such as a fed-ramp authorized S S E,
per-application V P N access lists,

132
00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:47.277
and named-location Conditional Access.

133
00:08:47.277 --> 00:08:50.772
Step two: Learn's operations
guidance is to back up the

134
00:08:50.772 --> 00:08:55.378
configuration through Graph exports
weekly and before every change.

135
00:08:55.378 --> 00:08:57.108
The guide hashes that export,

136
00:08:57.108 --> 00:09:01.280
and it becomes the configuration
evidence for every later step.

137
00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:08.380
Step three puts the client
on every managed device.

138
00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:14.339
It runs on Windows ten L T S C 2021 or
later and Windows eleven, on devices joined,

139
00:09:14.339 --> 00:09:17.598
hybrid joined or registered in the tenant;

140
00:09:17.598 --> 00:09:21.230
registered devices carry
only Private Access.

141
00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.725
It can't go on a machine that
hosts virtual machines with a

142
00:09:24.725 --> 00:09:28.368
Hyper-V external switch, and it
tunnels I P version four only.

143
00:09:28.368 --> 00:09:31.737
Package Learn's installation
script with the client,

144
00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:35.260
and create the Win32 app in
Intune with Learn's commands,

145
00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:39.855
detection rule and return codes,
assigned as required to the pilot group.

146
00:09:39.855 --> 00:09:43.906
Because the client doesn't
support secure D N S,

147
00:09:43.906 --> 00:09:47.530
turn off D N S over H T
T P S in Edge and Chrome.

148
00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:53.081
Learn's advice is to let users run unrestricted
first; from ring two, a remediation

149
00:09:53.081 --> 00:09:58.218
hides the disable and sign-out buttons
and restricts non-privileged users.

150
00:09:58.218 --> 00:10:01.766
Then count: the device usage
summary's active devices,

151
00:10:01.766 --> 00:10:07.130
divided by Intune's in-scope Windows
devices, is the client coverage metric.

152
00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.367
Steps four and seven belong together.

153
00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:17.200
The Microsoft traffic profile
acquires Microsoft three

154
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.793
sixty-five and Entra traffic,
and only that profile can.

155
00:10:20.793 --> 00:10:25.196
Enable it, assign the pilot group in
the portal, and turn on signaling,

156
00:10:25.196 --> 00:10:29.993
which carries source I P restoration,
so Entra sees the user's real address.

157
00:10:29.993 --> 00:10:35.593
Learn's test: a SharePoint sign-in shows
the same I P with the client on or off.

158
00:10:35.593 --> 00:10:39.467
The compliant network check then
passes only for users who come

159
00:10:39.467 --> 00:10:43.780
through this tenant's Global Secure
Access, and denies a replayed token.

160
00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:48.933
Learn's example policy goes straight to on;
the guide runs it in report-only for ten

161
00:10:48.933 --> 00:10:54.317
business days, excluding the Intune enrollment
apps and the emergency access group.

162
00:10:54.317 --> 00:10:56.092
Know the failure modes first.

163
00:10:56.092 --> 00:10:58.981
A disabled client is
blocked at the next sign-in.

164
00:10:58.981 --> 00:11:04.080
In an outage, Microsoft's break-glass
scripts switch the policies to report-only.

165
00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:07.618
Turning signaling off while
policies are on blocks users.

166
00:11:07.618 --> 00:11:10.890
And the check doesn't apply
to Private Access apps.

167
00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.615
Step five.

168
00:11:15.615 --> 00:11:20.210
Internet Access groups web content
filtering, threat intelligence,

169
00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:24.079
T L S inspection and file
policies into security profiles,

170
00:11:24.079 --> 00:11:27.465
and Conditional Access
assigns a profile to users.

171
00:11:27.465 --> 00:11:29.955
Learn's Graph tutorial is the procedure:

172
00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:35.085
a filtering policy that blocks the categories
the agency's acceptable-use policy

173
00:11:35.085 --> 00:11:38.027
names, a security profile,
the link between them,

174
00:11:38.027 --> 00:11:42.252
and a Conditional Access policy that
assigns it, scoped to the pilot.

175
00:11:42.252 --> 00:11:43.631
Enable both profiles,

176
00:11:43.631 --> 00:11:47.766
because the internet profile
doesn't include Microsoft traffic.

177
00:11:47.766 --> 00:11:52.015
Put threat intelligence in the baseline
profile so it covers everyone.

178
00:11:52.015 --> 00:11:55.759
Learn's limits: H T T P and
H T T P S on ports eighty

179
00:11:55.759 --> 00:11:59.704
and four forty-three, no I
P version six and no U D P.

180
00:11:59.704 --> 00:12:02.335
Quick isn't forwarded end to end,

181
00:12:02.335 --> 00:12:08.102
so the guide blocks U D P four
forty-three with a Windows firewall rule.

182
00:12:08.102 --> 00:12:12.440
And every bypass gets an
owner, a reason and an expiry.

183
00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:22.265
Steps six and ten replace the V P N's
address pool with one path per application.

184
00:12:22.265 --> 00:12:27.148
Learn's Graph tutorial creates each
application from the custom template,

185
00:12:27.148 --> 00:12:31.242
binds it to a connector group, and
adds only the segments it uses:

186
00:12:31.242 --> 00:12:33.290
one host, its ports, its protocol.

187
00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.372
Assign a group, not individuals,

188
00:12:35.372 --> 00:12:39.215
and put Conditional Access on
the app, in report-only first.

189
00:12:39.215 --> 00:12:43.665
Quick Access is the bridge: application
discovery shows what's used,

190
00:12:43.665 --> 00:12:47.877
and each piece moves into its own
app until Quick Access is empty.

191
00:12:47.877 --> 00:12:51.578
Step ten does the same for the
paths an attacker wants most.

192
00:12:51.578 --> 00:12:55.915
Administrative hosts get their own apps
on R D P, and their policy requires

193
00:12:55.915 --> 00:13:00.253
the phishing-resistant authentication
strength and a compliant device.

194
00:13:00.253 --> 00:13:03.178
Domain controllers, for
as long as they remain,

195
00:13:03.178 --> 00:13:06.952
get the Private Access sensor,
which installs in audit mode.

196
00:13:06.952 --> 00:13:11.933
Learn's advice: protect application S
P N's first and the domain controller's

197
00:13:11.933 --> 00:13:16.700
own later, to avoid locking out
administrators, and test with klist.

198
00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:21.988
Steps eight and nine.

199
00:13:21.988 --> 00:13:27.333
Universal tenant restrictions apply the
tenant restrictions version two policy to

200
00:13:27.333 --> 00:13:31.174
devices with the client, or on a
remote network, without a proxy,

201
00:13:31.174 --> 00:13:34.487
so users can sign in only to
tenants the policy allows.

202
00:13:34.487 --> 00:13:38.151
The policy comes first,
default block, then tagging.

203
00:13:38.151 --> 00:13:41.485
It reaches sign-in and the
Microsoft Graph data plane,

204
00:13:41.485 --> 00:13:44.137
not the SharePoint or Exchange data plane.

205
00:13:44.137 --> 00:13:46.690
Learn's four-case test checks a member,

206
00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:49.901
an allowed partner, a
blocked tenant, and a guest.

207
00:13:49.901 --> 00:13:55.400
T L S inspection is the step most likely
to break applications, so it comes last.

208
00:13:55.400 --> 00:14:00.207
Learn suggests a dedicated certificate
authority, not your production P K I.

209
00:14:00.207 --> 00:14:05.700
Deploy trust before activation, because an
expired certificate stops all inspection.

210
00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.622
The privacy officer approves
the scope, users are told first,

211
00:14:09.622 --> 00:14:13.740
and a failure rate above one
percent is a systemic problem.

212
00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:20.178
Steps eleven and twelve
make it a migration.

213
00:14:20.178 --> 00:14:22.565
Ring zero is the implementing team.

214
00:14:22.565 --> 00:14:27.290
Ring one is a pilot of fewer than
twenty users, for ten business days.

215
00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:31.740
Ring two is about ten percent,
including one branch, for twenty.

216
00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.498
Ring three is everyone, in waves,

217
00:14:34.498 --> 00:14:38.953
and a V P N route is removed
only after its wave holds.

218
00:14:38.953 --> 00:14:42.630
The emergency access group is
excluded from every policy,

219
00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:44.852
and tested at the start of each ring.

220
00:14:44.852 --> 00:14:48.831
Rolling back runs newest first:
policies to report-only,

221
00:14:48.831 --> 00:14:53.673
security profile links removed, the
wave's group out of the profiles,

222
00:14:53.673 --> 00:14:57.478
the V P N route restored, and
signaling turned off last.

223
00:14:57.478 --> 00:14:59.666
Telemetry comes before any of it.

224
00:14:59.666 --> 00:15:04.609
Learn keeps traffic logs in the service
for thirty days, so a diagnostic setting

225
00:15:04.609 --> 00:15:07.980
exports the four network
categories to the workspace,

226
00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.003
and a freshness query runs daily.

227
00:15:10.003 --> 00:15:13.721
A sudden drop in traffic
volume is an alert in itself:

228
00:15:13.721 --> 00:15:17.980
it covers offboarding, a
disabled profile, or an outage.

229
00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.593
To close, the dangers.

230
00:15:23.593 --> 00:15:28.868
Building in G C C High or D O D,
where the service isn't yet supported.

231
00:15:28.868 --> 00:15:33.293
Enforcing the compliant network before
the client is everywhere in scope.

232
00:15:33.293 --> 00:15:36.943
Catching the emergency access
accounts in a block policy.

233
00:15:36.943 --> 00:15:39.789
Leaving Quick Access
as a V P N-wide range,

234
00:15:39.789 --> 00:15:44.017
which is the old lateral movement
exposure under a new name.

235
00:15:44.017 --> 00:15:49.280
Issuing the T L S inspection certificate
from the authentication P K I.

236
00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:54.055
And retiring the V P N's web path
before CISA telemetry is agreed.

237
00:15:54.055 --> 00:15:55.892
Each has its guard in the steps.

238
00:15:55.892 --> 00:15:58.776
The guide is plain about
what it couldn't verify:

239
00:15:58.776 --> 00:16:01.957
government-cloud availability
feature by feature,

240
00:16:01.957 --> 00:16:05.137
assigning users to a traffic
profile through Graph,

241
00:16:05.137 --> 00:16:10.092
whether report-only policies apply security
profiles, and the T L S status values.

242
00:16:10.092 --> 00:16:11.793
Nothing was run in a tenant.

243
00:16:11.793 --> 00:16:16.869
To go further, read Network and Transport,
chapter five; the Conditional Access

244
00:16:16.869 --> 00:16:18.977
library in Access and Privilege;

245
00:16:18.977 --> 00:16:22.492
and Security Operations and
Telemetry, chapter three.

246
00:16:22.492 --> 00:16:23.310
Thank you.
